WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:15.070
On July 1st, 1997, after 155 years of British
sovereignty, Hong Kong returned to China.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:20.330
It did so as a special administrative region,
under the “1 country, 2 systems” model.

00:00:20.330 --> 00:00:25.270
A model whose goal was to ensure that Hong
Kong would continue being Hong Kong, the freest

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:27.950
city on the entire Asian continent.

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:31.850
That means that thanks to this particular
political model, Hong Kong has its own laws,

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:37.710
its own justice system, its own police force,
currency-- even its customs and immigration

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:41.180
policy is different from the rest of China.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:46.910
So in the day to day, it works almost like
an independent country.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:51.180
And it will remain that way until at least
the year 2047.

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.829
Maybe that's why, since Hong Kong passed into
Chinese hands, things in the city have gone

00:00:54.829 --> 00:00:58.530
pretty well.

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:03.109
Over the past 20 years, Hong Kong's economy
has grown by an average of 3.3% per year;

00:01:03.109 --> 00:01:07.610
more than 1 million people from Mainland China
have moved to this territory in search of

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.600
fortune; and the city is still an oasis of
freedom.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.750
Yes, the influence and interference of the
Chinese government in local politics has grown

00:01:17.750 --> 00:01:19.580
a lot in recent years.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:22.450
Here, on VISUALPOLITIK we already talked about
this.

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.700
For example, in past videos we discussed the
umbrella revolution, and Beijing’s growing

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:29.220
interference in local politics.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:34.090
Well, my friends, in this video we’ll talk
about yet another outstanding feature of this

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.090
place.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:40.299
Did you know that Hong Kong is the city with
some of the most expensive and smallest houses

00:01:40.299 --> 00:01:42.710
in the world?

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:46.790
Listen up.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:52.490
(A RISING MARKET)

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:57.270
With seven and a half million inhabitants
and more than 1,200 skyscrapers, homes in

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:59.630
Hong Kong are terribly expensive.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.500
Well, homes, garages, offices...

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.090
everything that has to do with the real estate
sector is... very expensive.

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:09.729
So much that we can find news like this:

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:15.870
( “Hong Kong parking spot sold for a record
US$600,000” SCPM)

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:19.950
In fact, if you had 1 million US dollars,
and you wanted to buy... let's say, an apartment

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:25.820
in one of the city’s nice areas, you’d
barely be able to afford 22m2 / 236 ft2 ... for

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:26.820
a million dollars!

00:02:26.820 --> 00:02:27.820
You heard that right.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:29.629
That is a scary figure.

00:02:29.629 --> 00:02:34.310
When considering cities with the most expensive
premium homes in the world, Hong Kong places

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:36.790
second, just behind Monaco.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:42.359
(“Alibaba chief buys his own Aladdin’s
cave: Jack Ma 'purchases Hong Kong’s most

00:02:42.359 --> 00:02:44.790
expensive home for US$193 million”.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:45.980
Daily Mail)

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:49.099
But of course, if we take into account that
all kinds of people live in Hong Kong and

00:02:49.099 --> 00:02:54.689
skip Monaco’s very particular case, Hong
Kong is by far the city with the most expensive

00:02:54.689 --> 00:02:59.620
homes in the world.

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:05.669
To give you an idea, since 2003 the price
of housing in Hong Kong has risen by 450%

00:03:05.669 --> 00:03:14.799
and today the average price per m2 exceeds
US $16,000; that is 1,486 USD/ft2 .

00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:18.970
Yes, that means that if you want to buy a
normal 90m2 house, nothing fancy, you’d

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:25.090
have to pay, at the very least, one and a
half million dollars.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:26.090
Quite a mortgage!

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:30.519
It’s very common to compare which cities
are more or less accessible by counting how

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:35.870
many years of average wage, before taxes,
we’d need to work to buy an average home.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:39.870
So let’s take a look.

00:03:39.870 --> 00:03:43.590
According to the latest published data, to
buy a house in Hong Kong we’d need more

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:46.720
than 18 years of full wages.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:52.239
For comparison, in cities like Tokyo or Singapore,
one needs less than 5 years of wages.

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:56.280
In other words, Hong Kong’s situation is
crazy.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:02.790
And not only that, since property is so expensive
and inaccessible for Hong Kong’s own citizens...

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:05.069
they’ve had to buy smaller and smaller homes.

00:04:05.069 --> 00:04:12.260
Do you know what the average surface area
for a home in Hong Kong is?

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:14.049
Come on guess.

00:04:14.049 --> 00:04:15.239
100 m2?

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:16.239
80?

00:04:16.239 --> 00:04:17.239
60?

00:04:17.239 --> 00:04:20.000
No way! 42m2, or about 452ft2

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:23.889
Can you imagine living in a 42m2 house?

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:26.700
Well, check out the latest trend:

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:31.980
( “Hong Kong is so expensive that architects
are building 100-square-foot ‘tube homes’

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.940
made from concrete water pipes”.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:34.940
World Economic Forum.)

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:37.240
But, okay, some of you may be thinking…

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.040
wow Simon, that’s very small but…

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:43.450
I’m sure it’s because a lot of people
live alone in tiny apartments, which alters

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.010
the statistics, right?

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:46.040
Well... no.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:51.570
On average, almost 3 people live in a 42m2
or 425ft2 apartment.

00:04:51.570 --> 00:04:53.150
That’s insane!

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:56.380
And, it’s not only the homes.

00:04:56.380 --> 00:05:02.690
The same thing happens with offices:

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:11.960
(“Hong Kong skyscraper sells for a record
US $5.2 billion” CNN)

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:14.790
But of course, having said that, you may be
wondering.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:19.460
Why the hell are homes so expensive in Hong
Kong?

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:25.690
Well... listen up.

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:30.710
(THE LACK OF LAND, A MYTH?)

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:32.630
Massive spaces, skyscrapers everywhere, small
apartments… and I mean small...

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:39.250
tiny and expensive... apparently, that's how
we could all describe Hong Kong, don’t you

00:05:39.250 --> 00:05:40.250
think?

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:47.400
After all, we’re talking about one of the
most densely populated places in the world.

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:51.770
Alright so, many people and very little space
pretty much the perfect combination for super

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:53.200
high prices, right?

00:05:53.200 --> 00:06:00.360
Well... hold on just a moment, because dear
viewer… isn’t the whole truth.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:05.670
The truth is that less than 25%, that is a
quarter of Hong Kong’s territory, is urbanized

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:10.480
and only 7% of the entire territory is occupied
by urban housing.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:16.200
Outside the crowded areas we can find countryside,
jungle, old villages, golf courses and many,

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:21.770
many single-family homes.

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:24.400
So how is this possible?

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:30.800
See, for years Hong Kong was considered the
freest economy on the entire planet by the

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.530
Index of Economic Freedom.

00:06:32.530 --> 00:06:37.120
We’re talking about a place with low taxes,
free trade, legal security and a very small

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:38.790
amount of government intervention.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:42.170
Yes, this is a highly capitalist city.

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:47.180
That’s why it’s the third largest financial
center in the world, after London and New

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:48.180
York.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:49.180
By the way, interesting fact.

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.140
The Basic Law, Hong Kong’s constitution,
limits the government so it can’t spend

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:54.140
more than it makes.

00:06:54.140 --> 00:06:57.840
This means that the government usually closes
every year with surpluses.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:03.670
It has accumulated so many surpluses that
it has saved, check this out, over 220 billion

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:06.250
dollars.

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:11.090
Yes, public debt doesn’t have much room
here.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:12.240
But... let's continue.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.780
The fact is that despite so much freedom and
so much commitment to capitalism...

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:21.720
there’s a sector in which the government
has intervened a lot.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.060
You know which?

00:07:26.060 --> 00:07:28.150
Precisely.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:29.240
The real estate sector.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:35.360
See, the government owns practically all the
land in Hong Kong.

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:42.020
Public authorities control when and how much
land is put on the market to be able to build

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.020
on.

00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:47.390
And of course, the Development Bureau decides
what can be built.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:53.260
Generally, land concessions are given through
auctions, in terms of private homes, offices

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:58.620
or commercial uses; or directly through agreements
with developers when public housing with lower

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:04.990
rental prices is going to be built, which
is where a large part of the population lives.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:13.360
And it’s normal, even in auctions, for the
property to continue being government property,

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.970
as they are granted a right of use for an
unspecified amount of years.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:22.850
So, sure, homes being so expensive in Hong
Kong has a lot to do with scarcity, but not

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:29.780
due to a geographical shortage-- due to the
government’s decision.

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:34.399
There’s plenty of land to develop new residential
and office areas.

00:08:34.399 --> 00:08:38.010
And, there’s always the option of gaining
ground at sea.

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:44.070
Yes, it isn’t a strange thing to do: a quarter
of all the built areas of Hong Kong are on

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:46.029
land that was claimed from the sea.

00:08:46.029 --> 00:08:50.079
But the government decides absolutely everything.

00:08:50.079 --> 00:08:52.990
(“The shortage of land is a myth.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:57.449
There are over 200 hectares of land available
for building in the urban areas.

00:08:57.449 --> 00:09:01.519
The shortage of housing is real but that is
because of deliberate manipulation of the

00:09:01.519 --> 00:09:02.970
market by the government.”

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:06.760
John Wright, a barrister and chairman of Friends
of Sai Kung.)

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:11.269
Well, in recent decades, as we mentioned before,
Hong Kong’s population has grown a lot because

00:09:11.269 --> 00:09:15.980
many people have come in from China; in addition,
family size has decreased, and many Chinese

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:21.360
investors want to invest in this city, known
for its legal security.

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:29.379
All this has led to a rise in demand… but
not supply… because the government didn’t

00:09:29.379 --> 00:09:30.970
want it to.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:34.699
“Home prices will continue to grow due to
limited supply”.

00:09:34.699 --> 00:09:38.380
Thomas Lam, Senior director at Knight Frank)

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:39.839
But...

00:09:39.839 --> 00:09:44.279
Why is the government interested in restricting
supply and increasing prices?

00:09:44.279 --> 00:09:46.700
Well... there are at least 2 reasons:

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:50.820
The first is that auctions are a great business
for the public purse.

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:56.300
In 2017, for example, 1 out of every 5 dollars
from public revenues came from land sales.

00:09:56.300 --> 00:10:01.290
We’re talking about more than 15 billion
dollars.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:06.760
The second reason... which is much more sinister,
is that there are suspicions of not very clean

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:10.559
relations between the authorities and large
promoters.

00:10:10.559 --> 00:10:15.360
Well, suspicions and even, convictions.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.889
( “Hong Kong Property magnate Thomas Kwok
back in jail for bribing city’s No 2 official”.

00:10:20.889 --> 00:10:21.889
SCPM)

00:10:21.889 --> 00:10:26.249
You see, the real estate sector in Hong Kong
is held in very few hands...

00:10:26.249 --> 00:10:27.870
hands that belong to multi-millionaires.

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:39.769
In fact, 7 of the 14 biggest estate magnates
in Asia are from this city.

00:10:39.769 --> 00:10:45.309
And well, these big businessmen prefer to
keep prices sky high and avoid competition...

00:10:45.309 --> 00:10:48.740
something that happens when the government
brings very little land to the market and,

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:49.759
in addition.

00:10:49.759 --> 00:10:52.029
increases any new project’s bureaucracy.

00:10:52.029 --> 00:10:56.829
That way, small and medium enterprises can’t
enter… and foreign companies don’t dare

00:10:56.829 --> 00:10:59.129
to, because they’d have a clear disadvantage.

00:10:59.129 --> 00:11:05.589
Of course, my friends, all of this has consequences.

00:11:05.589 --> 00:11:11.170
Listen up.

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:17.279
(THE CONSEQUENCES.)

00:11:17.279 --> 00:11:21.550
The crazy price of housing has meant that
despite having a lot of employment and high

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:27.639
wages, many people live in difficult conditions,
like families having to live in 40m2 or 431

00:11:27.639 --> 00:11:32.210
ft2 homes while paying exorbitant mortgages.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:37.589
This system has also led many people, especially
the youngest ones, to depend on the government’s

00:11:37.589 --> 00:11:41.460
public housing rentals... which of course
are also very small houses.

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:45.820
And remember, that in Hong Kong, there’s
no chance of living on the outskirts or in

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:49.910
more remote areas, because that would mean
crossing over to mainland China... and losing

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:54.509
all the freedoms that people have in this
city.

00:11:54.509 --> 00:11:58.860
And, as if that weren’t enough, the high
price of offices and business buildings has

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:03.579
caused many companies and many investments
to end up in other cities of the Pearl River

00:12:03.579 --> 00:12:10.230
Delta, such as Shenzhen, which poses problems
for the future.

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:12.589
But we’ll talk about that some other day.

00:12:12.589 --> 00:12:17.119
Anyway, my friends, that's how things are
in the city that, until now, has been one

00:12:17.119 --> 00:12:20.199
of the most prominent beacons of prosperity
and freedom in Asia.

00:12:20.199 --> 00:12:25.130
Only the future will tell us whether it will
continue to be, and you know what?

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:28.029
We’ll be very attentive here at VISUALPOLITIK.

00:12:28.029 --> 00:12:29.069
But now, it’s your turn.

00:12:29.069 --> 00:12:32.910
Do you think that the Hong Kong authorities
should promote the city’s growth by increasing

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:34.410
the real estate supply?

00:12:34.410 --> 00:12:37.519
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

00:12:37.519 --> 00:12:41.860
I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did and don’t forget to

00:12:41.860 --> 00:12:45.319
subscribe to our channel for brand new videos
every Monday and Thursday.

00:12:45.319 --> 00:12:49.149
Also, don’t forget to check out our friends
at the Reconsider Media Podcast - they provided

00:12:49.149 --> 00:12:51.740
the vocals in this episode that were not mine!

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:53.529
And as always, thanks for watching!

